+// Reported by the embedding application (Bun's execve(2) interposer) so
+// Thread::suspend()'s signal-based handshake can wait out a real execve(2)
+// currently in flight on this process before starting it. The two can
+// otherwise collide on HongMeng's kernel: the kernel's own thread-group
+// bookkeeping during execve(2) (the same window Bun's execve wrapper already
+// retries a concurrent pthread_create's EAGAIN for) overlaps with this
+// handshake's signal delivery / register capture, observed (real-device
+// faultlog) to hand the collector a torn snapshot of a thread that was never
+// actually suspended, later crashing with a null-pointer dereference from
+// JIT-generated code. Backed by a count, not a flag: more than one thread can
+// have an execve(2) in flight at once (each call is independently
+// Begin-then-End, matching Bun's own threads_in_execve counter), so the
+// window is only closed once every in-flight execve has ended.
+WTF_EXPORT_PRIVATE void reportExecveBegin();
+WTF_EXPORT_PRIVATE void reportExecveEnd();
+#endif
